1. INTRODUCTION TO QUEUE
============================================================

Queue is a linear data structure that follows FIFO.

FIFO = First In First Out.

The element inserted first will be removed first.

Real-Life Example:

People standing in a line for a ticket.

The person who comes first gets the ticket first.

Queue works on two ends:

1. FRONT:
   Points to the first element.
   Deletion takes place from FRONT.

2. REAR:
   Points to the last element.
   Insertion takes place from REAR.


DIAGRAM:

       FRONT                     REAR
         |                         |
         v                         v
       [10] ---> [20] ---> [30] ---> [40]

       Deletion                 Insertion


EXAMPLE:

Enqueue(10)
Enqueue(20)
Enqueue(30)

Queue:

       FRONT                REAR
         |                    |
         v                    v
       [10] ---> [20] ---> [30]


Dequeue()

Removed Element = 10

Queue:

                 FRONT      REAR
                   |          |
                   v          v
                 [20] ---> [30]


============================================================
2. QUEUE TERMINOLOGY
============================================================

1. FRONT:
   Points to the first element of the queue.

2. REAR:
   Points to the last element of the queue.

3. ENQUEUE:
   Operation used to insert an element into the queue.

4. DEQUEUE:
   Operation used to remove an element from the queue.

5. PEEK:
   Returns the front element without deleting it.

6. ISEMPTY:
   Checks whether the queue is empty.

7. ISFULL:
   Checks whether the queue is full.
   Mainly used in fixed-size array implementations.

8. OVERFLOW:
   Trying to insert an element when the queue is full.

9. UNDERFLOW:
   Trying to delete an element when the queue is empty.


