9. CIRCULAR QUEUE USING ARRAY
============================================================

Circular Queue is an improved version of Linear Queue.

In Circular Queue, the last index is logically
connected to the first index.

It follows FIFO.

MAIN PURPOSE:

To reuse empty spaces created after deletion.

We maintain:

front = -1
rear = -1


IMPORTANT FORMULAS:

NEXT POSITION:

(index + 1) % capacity

PREVIOUS POSITION:

(index - 1 + capacity) % capacity


A. INSERTION (ENQUEUE)

STEPS:

1. Check whether queue is full.

   (front == 0 && rear == capacity - 1)
   OR
   front == rear + 1

2. If queue is empty:
   front = rear = 0.

3. Otherwise:
   rear = (rear + 1) % capacity.

4. Insert element at arr[rear].


WHY USE MODULUS?

Formula:

rear = (rear + 1) % capacity

It moves rear to the next index.

If rear reaches the last index,
it automatically moves to index 0.

Example:

capacity = 5
rear = 4

rear = (4 + 1) % 5
rear = 0


B. DELETION (DEQUEUE)

STEPS:

1. Check whether front == -1.

2. Store arr[front].

3. If front == rear:
   Only one element is present.
   Set front = rear = -1.

4. Otherwise:
   front = (front + 1) % capacity.

5. Return removedElement.


C. EXAMPLE

Capacity = 5

Insert:

10, 20, 30, 40, 50

front = 0
rear = 4

Delete:

10, 20

front = 2
rear = 4

Insert:

60, 70

For 60:

rear = (4 + 1) % 5 = 0

For 70:

rear = (0 + 1) % 5 = 1


FINAL LOGICAL QUEUE:

FRONT                         REAR
  |                             |
  v                             v
[30] ---> [40] ---> [50] ---> [60] ---> [70]
  2         3         4         0         1

The queue uses indexes 2, 3, 4, 0 and 1.


TIME COMPLEXITY:

Enqueue = O(1)
Dequeue = O(1)
Peek = O(1)
Display = O(n)


