20. QUEUE USING TWO STACKS
============================================================

Queue follows FIFO.
Stack follows LIFO.

We can implement a Queue using two Stacks.

We use:

Stack 1 = Input Stack
Stack 2 = Output Stack


A. ENQUEUE

1. Push the new element into Stack 1.

B. DEQUEUE

1. If Stack 2 is not empty:
   Pop the top element from Stack 2.

2. If Stack 2 is empty:
   Transfer all elements from Stack 1 to Stack 2.

3. Pop the top element from Stack 2.


EXAMPLE:

Enqueue:

10, 20, 30

Stack 1:

TOP
 |
[30]
[20]
[10]

Stack 2:

EMPTY


Transfer all elements to Stack 2:

Stack 1:

EMPTY

Stack 2:

TOP
 |
[10]
[20]
[30]


Dequeue:

Removed Element = 10

This follows FIFO.


TIME COMPLEXITY:

Enqueue = O(1)

Dequeue = O(1) Amortized

A single dequeue can take O(n) when
all elements need to be transferred.


============================================================
21. QUEUE USING ONE STACK (RECURSION)
============================================================

A Queue can also be implemented using only one Stack
with the help of recursion.

ENQUEUE:

1. Push the new element into the Stack.

DEQUEUE:

1. If Stack is empty:
   Queue is empty.

2. Pop the top element.

3. If Stack becomes empty:
   This is the oldest element.
   Return it.

4. Otherwise:
   Store the popped element.

5. Recursively call dequeue().

6. Push the stored elements back while returning.


EXAMPLE:

Stack:

TOP
 |
[30]
[20]
[10]

The oldest element is 10.

Recursion removes 30, then 20, then 10.

After returning:

Push 20 and 30 back.

Removed element = 10.


TIME COMPLEXITY:

Enqueue = O(1)
Dequeue = O(n)

Auxiliary recursion space = O(n)


============================================================
22. STACK USING TWO QUEUES
============================================================

A Stack follows LIFO.
A Queue follows FIFO.

We can implement a Stack using two Queues.

Two approaches:

1. Push Costly
2. Pop Costly


A. PUSH COSTLY

Use:

Queue 1
Queue 2

STEPS:

1. Insert new element into empty Queue 2.

2. Move all elements from Queue 1 to Queue 2.

3. Swap Queue 1 and Queue 2.

The newest element always comes at the front
of Queue 1.

Push = O(n)
Pop = O(1)


B. POP COSTLY

STEPS:

1. Insert all elements into Queue 1.

2. During Pop, move all elements except the last
   from Queue 1 to Queue 2.

3. Remove the last element from Queue 1.

4. Swap both queues.

Push = O(1)
Pop = O(n)


============================================================
23. STACK USING ONE QUEUE
============================================================

A Stack can also be implemented using only one Queue.

APPROACH:

After inserting a new element:

1. Add the new element to the Queue.

2. Move all previous elements behind the new element
   by removing and adding them again.

3. The newest element comes to the front.

EXAMPLE:

Queue:

[10, 20]

Push 30:

Add 30:

[10, 20, 30]

Rotate previous elements:

[20, 30, 10]
[30, 10, 20]

Now 30 is at the front.

Pop removes 30.

TIME COMPLEXITY:

Push = O(n)
Pop = O(1)


